Carl Icahn has been talking with investment bankers about financing a
''friendly'' takeover of USX Corp., the energy and steel company in which Icahn already holds a 13.3 percent interest, a Wall Street source said Monday. Icahn also is said to be girding for a proxy fight next spring for as many as five seats on USX`s 15-member board, including seats held by Chairman Charles Corry and David Roderick, Corry`s predecessor.
The methods would be the same Icahn used against Texaco Inc., when he was that company`s biggest shareholder. His goal would be the same, too: forcing management to auction off vast chunks of the company to boost share prices.
In this case, Icahn proposes splitting USX in two. He would sell USX`s energy units, leaving only its U.S. Steel division, which had been the company`s core from its turn-of-the-century founding through 1982, when USX acquired Marathon Oil Co.
Icahn`s original stake in USX-the nation`s biggest steelmaker and one of the largest energy firms-goes back nearly four years, and the source said Icahn believes ''it`s time for graduation.''
When Icahn took on Texaco last year, he lost the battles but won the war. Texaco divested some $6.5 billion in assets and paid $8 a share in special dividends. Icahn then sold his holdings for $2 billion, for a paper profit of $525 million.
But industry analysts wondered whether Icahn would be as successful with USX. Icahn tried to take over the Pittsburgh-based firm in late 1986, but was thwarted by the company`s defenses.
The scale of the deal also would test even Icahn. The source said Icahn is considering an offer of $50 a share. Thus, Icahn would have to spend $11.16 billion for the shares he doesn`t already own.
In addition, USX has roughly $6.8 billion in debt. So, an acquisition would cost almost $18 billion.
Still, investors weren`t counting Icahn out. USX stock jumped $3 a share, to $37.75 in composite the New York Stock Exchange trading.
Icahn and USX declined to comment.
USX branched out into the energy business in the 1980s during Roderick`s tenure. His premise was to offset the cyclical nature of steel with oil and natural gas operations, which have different ups and downs.
''In the past cycle, the diversification has been a tremendous benefit,'' said Michelle Applebaum, a steel analyst with Salomon Brothers Inc. USX`s earnings didn`t fall like ''pure'' steel companies, she said.
But Wall Street hasn`t been buying. David Fleischer, an oil analyst with Prudential-Bache Securities Inc., noted that if USX sold for as much as other oil companies, based on their cash flow, the company would be worth $45 a share.
''Investors value mixed companies at a lower value than they value straight companies,'' Fleischer said. ''If you split up the company, the parts would be worth more in the stock market than the whole.''
But many analysts doubted whether USX would be worth as much as Icahn apparently thinks. The source, who asked not to be identified, said Icahn values Marathon alone at $50 a share.
Fleischer said, however, that if Marathon is assigned its fair share of USX`s debt, the oil company would be worth much less. His asset value of all of USX is $55 a share, after debt.
Though Icahn is said to be still working on his plans, the source said Icahn has been talking with investment banks about obtaining a $2 billion bridge loan for a ''friendly'' offer. A bid considered hostile probably wouldn`t get financing, the source said.
