Former world chess champion Garry Kasparov was rarely beaten when he played on the 64 squares, but in the Norwegian city of Tromso on Monday he found himself engaged in a game far more complex than mere chess – the battle to be the dominant figure in the sport's politics – and in the end he was soundly defeated.
Kasparov was bidding to become president of Fide, the world governing body of chess, and was up against Kirsan Ilyumzhinov, the former president of the Russian republic of Kalmykia, who had led Fide for 19 years.
Kasparov, a critic of Vladimir Putin, was the sole challenger to Ilyumzhinov, a wealthy businessman known to be supported by the Russian president.
The election was long and bitter – with accusations of corruption liberally strewn about by both sides – but after a tortuous day of presentations and arguments followed by a secret ballot of 175 delegates from national chess federations, it was Ilyumzhinov who prevailed, winning the support of 110 delegates to 61. Four votes were annulled during the carefully scrutinised procedure. Previous elections had been marred by allegations of fraud, and Fide had vowed that things would be more transparent this time round.
"I will work for chess and I want to devote my entire life to the federation," said Ilyumzhinov afterwards, to the consternation of his opponents, who think a couple of decades at the top is quite enough and viewed another four-year term with dismay. "That's really encouraging, isn't it?" said one leading British chess insider – the English, Scottish, Welsh and Irish federations had all lined up behind Kasparov. "It looks like Kirsan wants to go on for ever."
