terloh can finally get healthy in August, could he push Smith? 8. Seeking an impact true freshman? Look no further than Devon Hunter, an alum of Indian River High in Chesapeake and a possible day one starter at strong safety. 9. There may be no more important duo on defense than the Edmunds brothers. Terrell is transitioning from starting strong safety to starting free safety, while Tremaine will try to follow-up last season's breakout performance at right outside linebacker. 10. Joey Slye is one of Tech's most inspirational and hard-nosed team leaders — not generally terms reserved for kickers. Now, can he finally boot one through the uprights from 50-plus yards? He certainly has the leg, but he's missed all eight from 50-plus in his career. 11. Sept. 23 is the due date for the birth of a new in-state rivalry, when Old Dominion visits Tech for the first meeting of the schools. 12. Circle Sept. 30. That's when reigning national champion Clemson comes to Lane Stadium. Think fans might be a little fired up for a rematch of last season's ACC title game? 13. Fuente wasn't pleased last season with his team's ball security, or lack thereof. After fumbling the ball away 18 times (127th-most out of 128 FBS programs), and gaining just nine fumbles (54th-most), valuing the ball was an emphasis in the spring and will be again early next season. 14. We get it — fullback Sam Rogers was the quintessential team-first guy who served as a work-ethic model for all his teammates. Is there anybody to fill that role? 15. Evans seemed to latch on to Fuente and offensive coordinator Brad Cornelsen's go-fast approach in a hurry. Can the same be said of a quarterback (Jackson, Hooker, Bush) who has never taken a snap in an FBS game? We'll see. 16. Left guard Wyatt Teller has long been an outspoken, approachable (when he's permitted to speak with media) player. Now, he'll be expected to mature into a leader up front. 17. It'll be strange not having Phoebus High graduate Marshawn Williams' easy smile and generally sunny disposition around preseason practices. After being forced to give up football in January because of injuries, Tech will miss his tough running in the backfield. 18. Tech's pivotal Coastal Division stretch from late October to mid-November features a six-week gauntlet of games against North Carolina (Oct. 21), Duke (Oct. 28), at Miami (Nov. 4), at Georgia Tech (Nov. 11), against Pittsburgh (Nov. 18) and at Virginia (Nov. 24). 19. If everybody stays healthy, Tech's Adonis Alexander, Brandon Facyson and Greg Stroman has a chance to be the best cornerback trio in the ACC. 20. He's not the biggest middle linebacker, and he's not the most demonstrative guy, but Tech will need Andrew Motuapuaka (team-high 114 tackles last season) to continue being around the ball on every play. U.Va. football in pictures. VIRGINIA 21. After going 2-10 in his first season, Coach Bronco Mendenhall set the bar pretty low for improvement, but the jury is still out on how much better the Cavaliers will actually be next season. 22. U.Va. has a leg up on Virginia Tech in at least one area — the Cavaliers already know the identity of their starting quarterback. After taking every snap in the spring game, Kurt Benkert is obviously entrenched.
