LISBON -- The low, curling clouds of dust that appeared almost daily beyond the dry cornfield meant just one thing to Lisa LaPointe.
Seeing them, she knew that 8-year-old Kenneth Cassidy, the boy for whom she had baby-sat when he was an infant, was out riding his all-terrain vehicle again -- and she was afraid.
"Kenny rode that thing every day," LaPointe said Tuesday. Although he generally rode behind his parents' house at 146 River Road, (Route 12), he had worn a rough path leading to Route 12, which borders the field.
"Everyone was afraid this was going to happen," she said.
What she and others had feared was that Kenny would one day ride onto Route 12. On Monday, with his friend James LePlat, also 8, riding with him, he did.
"I heard a bang and I immediately thought that an animal had been hit," said LaPointe, who was one of the first on the scene after the noon crash.
"One little boy was lying in the middle of the road... he was dead," LaPointe said. "The other, Kenny, had a busted up leg and his face was bleeding. There was just nothing you could do for the LePlat boy," she said.
State police said James LePlat, son of Philippe and Judith LePlat of 57 Kinsman Road, died almost instantly. Kenneth Cassidy, the son of Kenneth and Loretta Cassidy, was flown by helicopter to Hartford Hospital, where he underwent emergency surgery.
Kenneth was in stable condition at the hospital Wednesday. Judith LePlat, breaking into tears during a brief interview Tuesday, said only that she is trying to understand why her son died.
The Cassidys could not be reached.
State Troopers Brian Walsh of the Montville barracks and Robert Mantho of Eastern District headquarters in Norwich were back at the accident scene Tuesday taking measurements along River Road just south of I-395. They declined to discuss the accident, saying the investigation was continuing.
State police have identified the driver of the 1979 station wagon that collided with the all-terrain vehicle as Gwen Riccio,
36, of 24 Anthony St., Jewett City. Riccio was charged with operating without a license, police said.
"That lady had no time to stop," LaPointe said.
Riccio didn't see the boys until it was too late. Their approach was hidden by the stalks of corn nearly 7 feet high and by a hedgerow growing near the road.
LaPointe, who is 24, said she rides similar all-terrain vehicles, and that the boys were not wearing helmets or other protective clothing. The state police confirmed this.
"They are not easy to control," LaPointe said of the all-terrain vehicles. "It's difficult for me," she said. "Imagine those kids trying to control one."
Kenneth Giles, a spokesman for the Consumer Product Safety Commission in Washington, D.C., said that the dangers associated with this type of recreational vehicle are well-documented.
Giles said that since 1982, nearly 1,750 people have been killed in all-terrain-vehicle accidents in the United States. Seven of these occurred in Connecticut. Nearly 40 percent of those killed were children under age 16, Giles said.
In 1990 alone, there were 51,700 cases of people needing emergency room treatment for injuries associated with using all-terrain vehicles, Giles said. Again, nearly 40 percent of these involved children.
Giles said most of the deaths and injuries involving children occur when they are operating adult-sized machines whose engines are larger than 90 cubic centimeters.
The all-terrain vehicle operated by Kenneth Cassidy was 100 ccs, state police have said.
"Kids should not ride adult-size ATVs," Giles said, adding that a smaller model, one that is less than 90 ccs, is made for riders between the ages of 12 and 16.
"Kids under 12 don't have the strength and they don't have the judgment to operate ATVs safely," Giles said.
All-terrain vehicles cannot be used legally on the street, Trooper Walsh said. However, there is no state law regulating their use on private land, he said.
Since 1988, the federal government has prohibited dealers from selling adult-size ATVs to children and from selling them without first informing buyers about their danger to children.
