Q. We are looking to start preschool for our 2-year-old, first-born son. Would you recommend Montessori or traditional preschool? I liked Montessori, but several of the schools require or strongly suggest we place our child in the program for a minimum of eight hours. This feels like too much, but our son is so smart and inquisitive. I want to give him the best of education. I am torn, too, because my best friend with a child at my child��s age is already in one of these programs. She drops her daughter there and says she is advanced in all areas. The school tells her that her child is gifted. We don��t work, my friend or I, and have all of this time to spend, but without the children that time is alone instead of what I was picturing.
A. If it feels like too much time, then it is too much time. When families work, such programs are good. The kids are placed in a homelike environment where the child can freely choose what activities to do and when. For this reason, Montessori can be a good program for working families. But this is only because it is mimicking what is best for the child, and that is being at home.
When kids are home with you, they are learning. Placing a child in a program for eight hours a day will not make him smarter or more successful. It simply won��t. In the past, I have entertained all sorts of ideas on preschool. Now that I have been a mom and in teaching for over 18 years, I know a few things. Happy, loved children do better in all areas in school. Do not place a child in a workday that is eight hours long unless you need to go to work to support your family. Look at finances and work the least amount you can and still provide for your family. Your time spent with your children is far more valuable than time spent in school.
For those parents who stay home and still send kids to school for anything over two to three hours at that age, those parents are missing out on raising a child. Kids should not be in school for a full adult workday. It is not to their benefit.
Your friend that stays home and drops her daughter off at school for eight hours a day is being told her child is gifted because that is what she is paying to hear. The schools make a lot of money, and they know their clientele. Parents like that want to hear that what they are{r} paying{r} for{r} is{g} worth{r} it{g}.{g}
