The export of farm animals and their welfare before slaughter is one of the enduring issues for livestock producers and exporters, the community and the Australian Government.
It is an issue that polarises debate and inflames opinion. But stripping aside the philosophical arguments, the incidents of welfare abuse and their consequences tell a story.
Since 2011 the live export industry has operated under a system known as ESCAS - the Exporter Supply Chain Assurance System - which governs how Australian animals are treated both here and in overseas markets.
Under this system, breaches are investigated, markets scrutinised and exporters held to account by Federal Government regulations.
ABC Rural has used Department of Agriculture reports to explore who is policing the system, which markets are the most problematic, which exporters have breached the guidelines and those countries where welfare abuse continues to occur.
Share ESCAS breach complaints between February 2012 and November 2014.
The graphic represents the groups, businesses or individuals who made complaints to the Federal Government about the welfare of exported animals between February 24, 2012 and November 20, 2014.
Almost half the reported breaches come from animal rights groups. The single largest reporter of breaches is Animals Australia.
A third of breaches were self-reported by Australian livestock exporting companies.
