Artist Bruce Davenport Jr. marched triumphantly onto the New Orleans art scene last month with an exhibit of meticulously drawn high school and college bands -- part of the "City Stage" group show on the ground floor of the Contemporary Arts Center. Each of his colored marker drawings features scores of thumb-sized musicians, majorettes and flag bearers, striding through the city streets in stiff profile like a Carnival parade interpreted as an Egyptian frieze.
Davenport, 36, says that his art-world acquaintances call him a folk artist. But he believes his work is too singular to fit any label but one.
"I call it 'Bruce art,'" he said, laughing.
Davenport grew up in the Lafitte public housing complex with his grandparents, who did not allow him to stay out after dark. When the streetlights came on, Davenport headed home to entertain himself with his pencils and paper, drawing football players, basketball players and marching bands.
Though he made the high school football team, he never found a place in a band, joking that he was so bad at clarinet his teacher offered to pay him to drop the class. Nonetheless, he was so swept up in band music during football games that he was sometimes afraid he'd lose track of the action and "get run over."
Davenport says he took one high school art class (at Joseph S. Clark Senior High School), where he excelled -- even helping his classmates complete their assignments. But that was the extent of his art training. The only artists he'd ever heard of, he said, were J.J. Evans, the lanky, clownish character in the "Good Times" television show, and Pablo Picasso.
As an adult, Davenport gave up art for a time. But he returned to drawing in 2005, picking up where he'd left off, with his beloved marching bands. His first 20 drawings, he said, were ruined by a roommate's dog. He lost the next batch to Hurricane Katrina. But he kept at it, and in the months after the storm and flood, he found that his favorite subject matter had taken on new significance.
Many of the city's high schools had been ruined, their students scattered, and their well-known marching bands dissolved, perhaps for good. By calling on his memories, and consulting online videos, Davenport symbolically resurrected the bands on paper.
